{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nDiscovered while driving `3276-2f81-8c75-4ddd` (cyclopean-bloodshot-hoatzin), the 2026-09-02 root-disk outage. That ticket's Defect 2 was a dead-man's-switch inversion: `rebar-root-disk-pressure` watched a metric published BY THE HOST THAT WAS DYING, under `treat_missing_data = \"notBreaching\"`, so CloudWatch read the publisher's silence as health and the alarm went GREEN at the moment the host became unreachable. Nobody was paged for 5 hours.\n\nThat was fixed for exactly TWO alarms. `root_disk_pressure` (monitoring_autodeploy.tf) and `gerrit_data_disk_high` (monitoring.tf) are now `breaching` on main and live. **Eleven other alarms in the same `rebar/host` namespace were left at `notBreaching`.**\n\n## The eleven, by blast radius\n\n1. **mirror_out_of_sync** (monitoring_ws7.tf) — WORST, and defective beyond host-death. Its publish sits inside `if [ -n \"$gerrit_sha\" ] && [ -n \"$github_sha\" ]`; the else branch logs and publishes NOTHING. So a LIVE, healthy host whose Gerrit REST call or `git ls-remote` breaks silently stops the signal and the alarm reads OK forever while GitHub main drifts. The Terraform comment calls this fail-safe; it is fail-OPEN. monitoring_ws7.tf itself states no other alarm covers mirror drift.\n2. **replication_errors** (monitoring_s5.tf) — same alive-host gap via a `[ -f \"$REPL_LOG\" ]` guard: a moved, renamed, or rotated-away replication log mutes the alarm permanently.\n3. **review_interrupts_signal_unavailable** (monitoring_autodeploy.tf) — this is the 'deploys are running blind' signal. Its documented archetype (bug 34cd) is a live-locked review gate sitting behind green alarms; muting it silently reproduces that.\n4. **deploy_errors** (monitoring_autodeploy.tf) — probe death means 'the box is not tracking main' goes unpaged.\n5. **voter_errors** (monitoring_s4b.tf), **review_bot_merge_change_errors** (monitoring_88ab.tf), **g2p_dispatch_errors** (monitoring_1fa8.tf) — both merge-gate legs (LLM-Review and Verified) go dark together.\n6. **mcp_retire_cap**, **mcp_mem_abort** (monitoring_foxterrier.tf), **review_interrupts_bound_exceeded** (monitoring_autodeploy.tf) — degradation rather than outage.\n7. **bridge_run_failures** (monitoring_bridge.tf) — also a heartbeat, but the ONLY one whose tradeoff is explicitly named and compensated: monitoring_bridge.tf:40-45 states the stop-entirely case is owned by the Reconciler Heartbeat Canary. Treat separately.\n\nFor 3-6 the pure host-down case is partly backstopped by `GerritReachable` and the two fixed disk alarms. The residual uncovered case for ALL of them is **the probe dying while the host lives** — a rebar-observability.timer failure, IAM/credential revocation, or `aws` erroring — which mutes ten alarms at once with nothing else firing.\n\n## Explicitly OUT of scope\n\n`monitoring_eb6e.tf:109` (Bedrock client-error rate) is correctly `notBreaching`: its source is an AWS-published Bedrock metric, genuinely absent when idle, with FILL(m,0) and a divide-by-zero guard. Any rule must be scoped to `namespace = \"rebar/host\"` so it stays out.\n\n## Tuning constraint — do NOT flip these blind\n\nThe risk is not false pages from sparseness (a healthy period publishes 0, so a breaching alarm stays OK). The risk is TIMER JITTER: a 2-hour sample showed ~22 of 24 periods present, so isolated missing periods are normal. Five alarms are `evaluation_periods = 1, datapoints_to_alarm = 1` (g2p_dispatch_errors, review_bot_merge_change_errors, replication_errors, and both review_interrupts_*) and WOULD FLAP on a single jittered tick. Flipping must be paired with `evaluation_periods >= 3`, matching how root_disk_pressure is configured (300s / 3 periods).\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [x] AC1: every `aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm` in namespace `rebar/host` either sets `treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"` or carries an explicit opt-out marker stating why. Verified by the guard test in AC4 passing with no unexplained exceptions.\n- [x] AC2 [REWRITTEN 2026-09-03 — the original form was defective]: the widened windows are proven not to flap by REPLAYING the recorded metric series against the new alarm configuration, showing zero state transitions across a window chosen for worst-case jitter. The original wording asked for a 24-hour observation period, which is waiting rather than verifying: it samples whichever day happens to elapse, proves nothing about a worse day, and cannot be re-run. Flap behaviour is DETERMINISTIC given a metric series and an alarm config, so it is answerable immediately and repeatably. Verified by replaying each flipped alarm's own recorded series (the pre-change 2-hour sample showed ~22 of 24 periods present, so isolated missing periods are the normal condition to replay against) and reporting the transition count per alarm.\n- [x] AC3: `mirror_out_of_sync` publishes a THRESHOLD-CROSSING value on its else branch (the branch taken when the Gerrit REST read or `git ls-remote` fails) instead of publishing nothing. Given the alarm's `threshold = 0` with `GreaterThanThreshold`, that value is `1`. Publishing `0` would NOT close the gap: it reads as in-sync and preserves exactly the fail-open this ticket is about. Flapping on a transient blip is suppressed by the alarm's existing `datapoints_to_alarm = 2` together with AC2's widened window — that blip risk is precisely what the current comment in `infra/scripts/observability.sh` (\"On a fetch failure we publish NOTHING (the alarm treats missing data as healthy) rather than risk a false alarm from a blip\") was written to avoid, so that comment must be rewritten in the same change to state the new disposition. Verified by inducing a fetch/REST failure and observing the alarm actually reach ALARM state, NOT merely by observing that some datapoint appeared.\n- [x] AC4: the rule is encoded as a test, not left to review vigilance — `tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py` already asserts the disk-alarm case and already has `_TREAT_MISSING_DATA_RE` plus an `# rebar:allow-actionless-alarm:` opt-out convention, so this is a small extension of an existing guard, not new machinery. Verified by the test failing on a deliberately reverted alarm.\n- [x] AC5 [non-codebase]: `bridge_run_failures` is explicitly decided — either left notBreaching with its existing documented compensating control, or flipped with an evaluation window >= 3 hours. Verified by a comment recording the decision.\n- [x] AC6 [non-codebase]: the false \"missing data means healthy\" rationale is corrected wherever it appears. It is NOT a single phrase and a one-phrase grep will not find it — it takes three distinct forms: (a) \"no X published in a period is the healthy steady state\", in monitoring_1fa8.tf:74, monitoring_s5.tf:50, monitoring_s4b.tf:78 and monitoring_88ab.tf:57 (with a cross-reference in monitoring.tf); (b) \"the probe fails safe\", in monitoring_ws7.tf:48-49; and (c) ABSENT ENTIRELY — monitoring_foxterrier.tf:68 and :104 carry a bare `treat_missing_data = \"notBreaching\"` with no justifying comment at all, so there is nothing to correct and a rationale must be ADDED. Verified by enumerating all eleven alarms individually and confirming each one either carries a corrected rationale or an explicit opt-out marker — not by grepping for one phrase.\n\n## Testing\n\n- `python -m pytest tests/unit/test_alarm_actions_terraform.py -q`\n- `terraform plan` showing only the intended alarm changes\n- post-apply: `aws cloudwatch describe-alarms` confirming the new settings, and a 24h flap check\n\n(Moved to ticket comments as EVIDENCE rather than plan: the sparse-counter measurement and\nAC2's replay tables. The 'eleven' enumeration below is retained but is now an UNDERCOUNT --\nthe current tree has 22 rebar/host alarms; see the close-verification comment.)"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788575644589597001,"uuid":"d301b331-b52f-4dc3-aa6b-5f1f0aed9611"}